nawk -- Command

Pattern-scanning language, the nawk dialect
nawk [-F c] [-f progfile] [prog] [var=value] [file ...]
awk  [-F c] [-f progfile] [prog] [var=value] [file ...]

nawk scans each input file for records that match a pattern and performs
an action on the ones that do.  A program is a sequence of

	pattern { action }

rules; a rule with no pattern acts on every record, and a rule with no
action prints the record.  Records are lines by default and fields are
the white-space-separated words within them, available as $1, $2 and so
on, with $0 the whole record.  With no file, or with the file name `-',
nawk reads the standard input.

ON THIS SYSTEM awk IS nawk.  /bin/awk and /bin/nawk are two names for one
file -- a hard link, so ls -l shows a link count of 2 and one size -- and
the program behind both names is the one described here.  It is NOT the
1985 Mark Williams awk that this Lexicon's awk article describes.  See
THE TWO awks below for what that changes.

OPTIONS
-F c		Use c as the field separator instead of white space.
		More than one character is taken as a regular expression:
		-F'[0-9]' splits on any digit.
-f progfile	Read the program from progfile instead of from the
		command line.  Repeat it and the last one wins.

An operand of the form var=value assigns to a variable before the files
after it are read, and is not itself a file name.

THE LANGUAGE
Patterns may be regular expressions between slashes, expressions,
comparisons, a pair of expressions separated by a comma (a range), or the
words BEGIN and END, whose rules run before the first record and after
the last.

Actions are C-like: if/else, while, for, for (var in array), do ... while,
break, continue, next, exit [expr], return, and { } to group.  Expressions
have the C operators + - * / % ^ ++ -- = += -= *= /= %= ^= ?: && || !, the
comparisons < <= == != >= >, the regular-expression matches ~ and !~, and
concatenation, which is written by putting two values next to each other.
Variables are numbers or strings as the context needs; an unset variable
is both "" and 0.

Arrays are associative and subscripted by strings: a["x"], and a[i,j] for
a multiple subscript, which is joined with SUBSEP.  `k in a' is true when
a has that subscript, `for (k in a)' walks them in no defined order, and
`delete a[k]' removes one.

Built-in variables: NR, FNR, NF, FS, OFS, ORS, RS, FILENAME, SUBSEP,
OFMT, RSTART, RLENGTH, ARGC and ARGV.  Assigning to a field rebuilds $0
with OFS between the fields; assigning past NF extends the record.  RS
set to a single character makes that the record separator; RS set to the
empty string makes a blank line one, so a record is a paragraph.

Built-in functions, all verified against this binary:

	length(s)  index(s,t)  substr(s,m[,n])  split(s,a[,fs])
	sub(re,repl[,target])  gsub(re,repl[,target])  match(s,re)
	sprintf(fmt,...)  getline  system(cmd)  close(file)
	sqrt  exp  log  sin  cos  atan2  int  rand  srand

sub and gsub replace the first and every match, return how many they
replaced, and default to $0.  match sets RSTART and RLENGTH.

print writes its arguments separated by OFS and ended by ORS; printf
takes a format and honours %d %i %o %x %c %s %e %f %g with field width,
precision, `-' and `0'.  Either may be redirected with > or >> to a file,
or with | to a command; getline reads the next record, `getline var'
reads into a variable, `getline < file' and `getline var < file' read
from a file, and `"cmd" | getline' reads from a command.  close() closes
any of them so it can be reopened.

Functions are defined at the top level and may recurse:

	function name(arg, arg, local, local) { ... return expr }

Parameters past the ones the caller supplies are the function's local
variables, which is the only way to declare one.  Arrays pass by
reference, scalars by value.  Read the LIMITS below before writing a
deeply recursive one.

THE TWO awks
There have been two awk languages on this machine and they are different
programs, not two releases of one.

The 1985 Mark Williams awk terminates a rule with a NEWLINE and nothing
else, so `awk '{n++} END{print n}'' -- the one-liner every tutorial opens
with -- is a syntax error to it, and it has no user-defined functions, no
split, gsub, substr, sprintf or getline, and integer division (1/3 is 0).
That is the language the awk article in this manual describes, and it is
what `stock' installs.

nawk is the 1989 Hirabayashi implementation of the newer awk the POSIX
1003.2 draft standardised.  It accepts the one-liner, has everything
listed above, and 1/3 is 0.333333.  Everything the older language did, it
does the same way: all nine programs of the port's own rule-separator test
produce byte-identical output under both.

So the awk article is not wrong about this program -- it is incomplete.
What it documents, nawk does; what it does not mention, nawk adds.  Where
the two disagree at all is the arithmetic (real division, and printf %x
taking its argument correctly), and there nawk is the one that is right.

LIMITS
	Characters per input record	511
	Fields per record		99
	Nested function calls		about 5

A record of 512 characters or more is refused with `record too long'
rather than truncated.  Records arrive in fixed 512-byte buffers
throughout this program; the check exists because without it a long line
walked off them and hung nawk with no output at all.

The nesting limit is not the language's and not this program's: a process
on this system runs on the 4 KB of stack it is given and the stack does
not grow, so any program dies of a segmentation violation about 4 KB
down.  Each nested nawk call costs the best part of a kilobyte of it, so
a function that recurses more than about five deep is killed.  Iteration
has no such limit -- loops, arrays and the record loop are all bounded by
memory instead, and a 2,000-line input with 2,000 distinct array
subscripts runs without trouble.

