The misspelling of the first name is theirs.  The regular-expression
package inside it is Henry Spencer's of 1986, from the University of
Toronto, modified by K. Hirabayashi and marked as modified, which is what
Spencer's terms ask of anyone who changes it.

Differences from the language as POSIX defines it, all measured here:

	int() rounds down rather than toward zero, so int(-3.7) is -4
	where awk and POSIX give -3.

	system() returns 1 if the command ran and 0 if it could not be
	started, rather than the command's exit status.

	toupper and tolower are not implemented, and neither is the -v
	option.  The 1985 awk had no -v either.

	Bracket expressions are ranges and lists only: [a-z] and [abc]
	work, the POSIX class names [[:upper:]] do not.  This regular
	expression package is Henry Spencer's of 1986 and predates them.

	`print (expr), expr' is a syntax error -- a parenthesised first
	argument followed by a comma.  Write it without the parentheses,
	or parenthesise the whole list.

	Assigning a field its own value, $1=$1, does not rebuild $0 with
	the current OFS.  Assigning any other value does.

The C library underneath contributes three of its own, and they are not
this program's doing.  printf has no %+ flag.  sin() is not exactly zero
at zero -- though compare `awk', which gets sin(1) and cos(1) wrong
altogether.  And a number that is not a whole number prints through OFMT,
whose default %.6g does not strip trailing zeros here, so `print 0.5'
says 0.500000; awk has printed it that way since 1985.  Whole numbers are
exact and print as integers.

The program understands Shift-JIS Japanese text, which is what the
authors wrote it for.  It cannot fire on 7-bit input.  On 8-bit input
that is not Shift-JIS the bytes 0x81-0x9F and 0xE0-0xFC are read as the
first half of a two-byte character.
